WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.640
A rabbit eats some of its own droppings.

2
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.340
Why would an animal do that?

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.820
The answer lies in the shape of the gut itself.

4
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:16.803
Many animals, from earthworms to us, have a complete gut.

5
00:00:18.043 --> 00:00:21.703
It is a tube, with a mouth at one end and an anus at the other.

6
00:00:22.943 --> 00:00:24.563
Food moves one way along it.

7
00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:28.323
So each stretch can do its own job, in order.

8
00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:31.363
The mouth and stomach break food up.

9
00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:37.483
At the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, bile and pancreatic juice

10
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:38.483
pour in.

11
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:43.727
Digestion goes on along the small intestine, the main absorbing stretch.

12
00:00:45.187 --> 00:00:47.507
Enzymes there do most of the digesting.

13
00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:51.487
Transporters in the wall carry the small pieces across.

14
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.770
Simple sugars and amino acids cross into blood capillaries.

15
00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:03.090
Most fat is packed into tiny particles that enter the lacteal, a small lymph vessel.

16
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.090
That is the first half of a rule.

17
00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:12.370
Large food molecules must be broken down before or within the stretch that can

18
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.370
absorb them.

19
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.540
Here is the second half.

20
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.200
Whatever a stretch does not absorb moves on.

21
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.740
Watch the water. The small intestine takes up most of it.

22
00:01:25.683 --> 00:01:27.903
The colon takes up most of what remains.

23
00:01:29.323 --> 00:01:32.983
Only a small leftover reaches the end, and it leaves in the feces.

24
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:35.767
Now feed the tube grass.

25
00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:42.767
Its main fiber, cellulose, has bonds no vertebrate is known to break with enzymes of

26
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:47.627
its own. Microbes do it, by fermenting the fiber without oxygen.

27
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.527
Fermentation makes short-chain fatty acids.

28
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:57.047
The fermenting chamber's own wall absorbs them on the spot, wherever that chamber

29
00:01:57.047 --> 00:01:58.047
sits.

30
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.690
But the microbes themselves are food, rich in protein.

31
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.650
The acid stomach starts digesting protein.

32
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:12.190
The small intestine finishes the job, and absorbs the amino acids.

33
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.813
A cow ferments in front of its acid stomach.

34
00:02:17.193 --> 00:02:21.353
Its microbes wash onward, into the acid and then the small intestine.

35
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:23.793
There they are digested.

36
00:02:24.833 --> 00:02:29.073
A horse ferments in its cecum and colon, after the small intestine.

37
00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:35.453
Its microbes are full of protein, and its small intestine is already behind them.

38
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:37.853
Where does that protein go?

39
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.260
Most of it leaves in the droppings.

40
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.080
The stretch that absorbs amino acids is already behind it.

41
00:02:52.083 --> 00:02:56.583
A rabbit ferments mainly in its cecum, after the small intestine too.

42
00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:00.583
So it makes soft pellets from its cecum, and eats them.

43
00:03:01.803 --> 00:03:05.503
The microbes go round again, through the stomach and small intestine.

44
00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:09.277
That is why a rabbit eats some of its droppings.

45
00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:14.757
Food must be broken down before or within the stretch that can absorb it.
